questions.question_body:
"I understand the authors model generating a dense rational factor matrix \(\mathbf R = B_m B_{m-1}\dots B_1\) as information flowing over a grid graph where nodes correspond to columns.
Could you detail:
(i) how each element \( [B_i]_{jk}\) maps onto directed edges;
(ii) what strictly needs the connectivity graph satisfy so that every source node reaches every sink node;
(iii) why counting edges gives a lower bound on trainable non‑zero entries?
An explicit small‑dimensional illustration would really help.

"Section 3 defines ¬\(B(k)=\tmatrix{{}\)}¬ with four block‑diagonal sub‑matrices. The authors say rotating every underlying 2×2 sub‑matrix makes the larger component automatically orthonormal.
Could someone clarify:
• How permuting those blocks preserves column‑orthogonality;
• What role Cayley transform plays here;
• And why additional non‑zero weights beyond those in *complete* legendary eight non‑zero pattern don’t destroy total unitary property?

"Discussion stated taking products like B₁…ᵗᵐ … B_d yields complete coverage analogous to Kaleidoscope hierarchy.
Could someone outline:
• Condition under which repetitive application restores arbitrary elements of O(d);
• Why practical settings cannot reach *exact* universal behaviour while keeping param budgets low;
• Maybe reference number m required relative d such that rank/dimension fit.
